// threshold_custody's init argument. TRACKED IN GIT, unlike icp_ledger_init.did.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `dfx deploy` failed on the operator's host 2026-10-07:
//
//     Failed to install wasm module to canister 'threshold_custody'.
//     Caused by: Failed to create argument blob.
//     Caused by: Invalid data: Expected arguments but found none.
//
// threshold_custody/threshold_custody.did declares `service : (KeyConfig) -> { ... }`
// and src/lib.rs has `#[ic_cdk::init] fn init(config: KeyConfig)`, so the canister
// REQUIRES an argument at install. dfx.json named none, so `dfx deploy` with no
// canister argument could never install it -- the ledger alone deployed fine, which
// is why this went unnoticed: every ICP measurement so far needed only the ledger.
//
// AN init_arg_file RATHER THAN AN INLINE init_arg, for a reason that is about this
// comment: dfx.json is JSON and JSON has no comments. The reasoning below has to live
// somewhere a reader will find it, and candid text files take `//`. It also matches
// what icp_ledger_canister already does, so there is one pattern here and not two
// (rule 8).
//
// TRACKED, AND THE LEDGER'S IS NOT, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE POINT.
// icp/icp_ledger_init.did is gitignored (.gitignore:116) because it carries ACCOUNT
// IDENTIFIERS, which are environment state: the same identity has a different account
// on every fresh replica, so a tracked copy would claim to know something only a
// running replica can answer. This file carries a KEY NAME, which is the same string
// on every local replica there has ever been. Nothing here goes stale when the
// container is recreated, so tracking it means `dfx deploy` works on a fresh checkout
// without a generation step.
//
// dfx_test_key IS LOCAL-ONLY AND THAT IS NOT A LIMITATION OF THIS FILE. It is the
// threshold ECDSA key name a local replica provides; mainnet's is key_1 and a test
// subnet's is test_key_1. A canister deployed to mainnet needs that value changed, and
// DFINITY's own documentation says dfx.json "does not support referring to values
// through environment variables. Values must be hardcoded in plain text" -- so the
// change would be to this file, deliberately, by whoever deploys there. Nothing in
// this tree deploys to mainnet, and icp/dfx.json's icp_ledger_canister entry carries
// `remote.id.ic` naming the real ledger precisely so dfx refuses to create one there.
//
// WHAT READS IT: icp/dfx.json's threshold_custody.init_arg_file. Nothing else.
(record { key_name = "dfx_test_key" })
